##DESCRIPTION
##  Mental Math problems for subtration.
##ENDDESCRIPTION

##KEYWORDS('mental math', 'subtraction')

## DBsubject('Arithmetic')
## DBchapter('Integers')
## DBsection('Addition/Subtraction')
## Author('Peter Staab')
## Institution('Fitchburg State University')
## Date('5/3/2020')

########################################################################

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('LimitedNumeric');

$a1   = random(50, 80);
$k1   = random(1,  2);
$a2   = 10 * random(2, 4) - $k1;
$ans1 = Compute($a1 - $a2);

$b1   = random(2, 4) * 100 + random(20, 60);
$k2   = random(2, 4);
$b2   = 100 - $k2;
$ans2 = Compute($b1 - $b2);

$d1   = random(12, 19);
$d2   = random(2,  9);
$c1   = random(6,  8) * 10 + $d1;
$c2   = random(2,  4) * 10 + $d2;
$ans3 = Compute($c1 + $c2 - $d1 - $d2);

BEGIN_PGML

Answer the following using Mental math techniques.

a. [`[$a1]-[$a2]=\;`] [__]{$ans1}
b. [`[$b1]-[$b2]=\;`] [__]{$ans2}
c. [`[$c1]+[$c2]-[$d1]-[$d2]=\;`] [__]{$ans3}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) Note that the subtrahend (second number) in the subtraction is near a multiple of ten. We can use compensation for this operation and add [$k1] to both numbers. 
[``` [$a1]-[$a2] = ([$a1]+[$k1])-([$a2]+[$k1]) = [$a1+$k1]-[$a2+$k1] = [$ans1] ```]

b) In this case, the subtrahend is close to 100, so we add [$k2] to both
[``` [$b1]-[$b2] = ([$b1]+[$k2])-([$b2]+[$k2]) = [$b1+$k2]-[$b2+$k2] = [$ans2]```]

c) In this case, notice that there are compatible numbers and if we rearrange the calculation is easier. 
[``` \begin{aligned}
[$c1]+[$c2]-[$d1]-[$d2] & = ([$c1]-[$d1])+([$c2]-[$d2]) \\
& = [$c1-$d1] + [$c2-$d2] = [$ans3]
\end{aligned}
```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION
ENDDOCUMENT();
